Alembic migrations for MemoryMap AI.

**Read `core/database.py`'s `_ensure_alembic_baseline` docstring first.**
This exists alongside `DatabaseManager`'s own additive auto-migrator
(`_add_missing_columns`), not instead of it — a plain new column still goes
through that, unchanged. This is the tool for a real *rename* or *drop*,
which the additive migrator was never able to do.

Every database this app opens, existing or brand new, gets stamped to the
current head revision at startup — `_ensure_alembic_baseline` runs
`command.stamp(cfg, "head")` (not `upgrade`) the first time it sees a
database with no `alembic_version` table, because that database's schema
already matches head by construction (`create_all()` + the auto-migrator
built it). Only once a database is already stamped does a later startup
take the `command.upgrade(cfg, "head")` path — a no-op until a real new
migration exists.

**To add a real migration** (a rename or drop):

1. Change the model in `core/database.py` the normal way.
2. `PYTHONPATH=src alembic revision --autogenerate -m "describe the change"`
   — point `MEMORYMAP_DATA_DIR` at a *copy* of a database with the old
   schema first, so autogenerate has something to diff against; against an
   empty or already-current database it will generate nothing.
3. **Read the generated migration.** Autogenerate does not reliably detect
   a rename as a rename — it is very likely to see "drop column A, add
   column B" instead, which loses every existing value in that column. Edit
   it by hand to use `op.alter_column(..., new_column_name=...)` when that
   is really what happened.
4. Test the migration against a copy of a real (or realistic) pre-change
   database, not just a fresh one — `alembic upgrade head` should leave
   existing data intact and correctly transformed.
5. Add a test alongside `tests/test_alembic_baseline.py`'s existing ones
   that builds a pre-migration database by hand and asserts the migrated
   result is correct.

`render_as_batch=True` is already set in `env.py` — SQLite can't `ALTER`
most column properties in place, so every migration that touches an
existing column needs Alembic's batch mode (rebuild-and-swap) to do it at
all. This is set once there rather than per-migration.
